'''Ahmed Gomaa Mohamed''' (10 March 1963 – 14 December 2013) was an Egyptian [[Paralympic powerlifting|Paralympic powerlifter]] and weightlifter. An icon of Egyptian Paralympic sports, he is one of the few athletes to have competed in five consecutive Paralympic Games from 1988 to 2004, winning a total of five medals (three gold, two silver).<ref name="ipc-results">{{cite web |title=Historical Results Archive – Powerlifting |url=https://www.paralympic.org/results |website=International Paralympic Committee |language=en |access-date=26 September 2026}}</ref>

His name appears in international result records in various formats, including ''Ahmed Mohamed Gomaa'', ''Ahmed Gomaa Mohamed'', and ''Gomma G. Ahmed''.

== Early life ==
Ahmed Gomaa Mohamed was born on 10 March 1963 in the city of [[Faqous]], [[Al Sharqia Governorate]].

== Sports career ==
Ahmed Gomaa had an exceptionally long and successful career in Paralympic sports, transitioning from weightlifting to powerlifting. 

=== Seoul 1988 ===
Competing under the name Gomma G. Ahmed, he made his Paralympic debut at the [[1988 Summer Paralympics|1988 Seoul Games]] in the men's weightlifting event. He won the silver medal in the -51 kg weight class.<ref name="ipc-1988">{{cite web |title=Seoul 1988 – Weightlifting results |url=https://www.paralympic.org/seoul-1988/results/weightlifting/mens-51-kg |website=International Paralympic Committee |language=en |access-date=26 September 2026}}</ref>

=== Barcelona 1992 ===
At the [[1992 Summer Paralympics|1992 Barcelona Games]], Gomaa transitioned to powerlifting and won the gold medal in the men's 52 kg weight class.<ref name="ipc-results" /> On 5 September 1992, he lifted 177.50 kg in the 52 kg bodyweight category, setting a new Paralympic and world record recognized by the [[International Paralympic Committee]].<ref name="ipc-1992-diploma">{{cite web |title=Barcelona 1992 – Powerlifting results |url=https://www.paralympic.org/barcelona-1992/results/powerlifting |website=International Paralympic Committee |language=en |access-date=26 September 2026}}</ref>

=== Atlanta 1996 ===
Moving up to the 56 kg weight class, Ahmed Gomaa successfully defended his Paralympic title at the [[1996 Summer Paralympics|1996 Atlanta Games]], securing his second consecutive Paralympic gold medal.<ref name="ipc-results" />

=== Sydney 2000 ===
At the [[2000 Summer Paralympics|2000 Sydney Games]], he won his third consecutive Paralympic gold medal in the 56 kg weight class, lifting 192.5 kg. By doing so, he broke his previous Paralympic and world records, cementing his dominance in the sport.<ref name="ahram-2000">{{cite news |title=Eat your hearts out |work=[[Al-Ahram Weekly]] |date=2000 |issue=507 |url=http://weekly.ahram.org.eg/Archive/2000/507/sp1.htm |language=en |access-date=26 September 2026}}</ref> During his career, he even reportedly reached lifts as high as 193.5 kg in international competition.

=== Athens 2004 ===
He participated in his fifth and final Paralympic Games at [[2004 Summer Paralympics|Athens 2004]], winning the silver medal in the 56 kg weight class by lifting 180.0 kg, finishing behind China's Wang Jian, who lifted 185.0 kg. The competition took place on 22 September 2004.<ref name="ipc-2004">{{cite web |title=Athens 2004 – Powerlifting, men's 56 kg |url=https://www.paralympic.org/athens-2004/results/powerlifting/mens-56-kg |website=International Paralympic Committee |language=en |access-date=26 September 2026}}</ref>

== Legacy and Death ==
Ahmed Gomaa Mohamed belongs to the generation that cemented [[Egypt at the Paralympics|Egypt's]] position as one of the most powerful nations in Paralympic powerlifting since the 1990s. Beyond his medals, he was highly respected for his sportsmanship and dedication, often acting as a mentor and coach for younger generations of Egyptian athletes.<ref name="elwatan-2013-legacy">{{cite news |title=وفاة بطل رفع الأثقال البارالمبي أحمد جمعة (Death of Paralympic powerlifting champion Ahmed Gomaa) |work=[[El Watan (Egypt)|El Watan]] |date=15 December 2013 |url=https://www.elwatannews.com/news/details/377767 |access-date=26 September 2026 |language=ar}}</ref>

Ahmed Gomaa passed away on the morning of Saturday, 14 December 2013, after a battle with illness.<ref name="elwatan-2013-legacy" /> The Egyptian press and athletic community mourned him, describing him as a historic champion who quietly paved the way for every Egyptian weightlifter of determination who came after him.<ref name="youm7-2013">{{cite news |title=وفاة بطل الأثقال الباراليمبى أحمد جمعة (Death of Paralympic weightlifting champion Ahmed Gomaa) |work=[[Youm7]] |date=15 December 2013 |url=https://www.youm7.com/story/2013/12/15/%D9%88%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%A9-%D8%A8%D8%B7%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A3%D8%AB%D9%82%D8%A7%D9%84-%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%8A%D9%85%D8%A8%D9%89-%D8%A3%D8%AD%D9%85%D8%AF-%D8%AC%D9%85%D8%B9%D8%A9/1398365 |access-date=26 September 2026 |language=ar}}</ref>

